To effectively hunt for bugs without drowning in thousands of identical responses, you must master filtering false positives and setting up instant notifications.
## Part 1: Automated False Positive Filtering with FFUF
When fuzzing thousands of historical URLs, many pages will return a 200 OK status code but display a generic "Not Found" or "Error" page. These are false positives. ffuf allows you to strip these out dynamically using filter switches.
## 1. Automatically Filter by Response Size (-fs)
If a server returns the exact same page size for every invalid payload, you can tell ffuf to ignore that specific size.

* Run a quick baseline test against a known bad page on the target:

curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{size_download}\n" "https://target.com"

* If it outputs 4125, append -fs 4125 to your ffuf command to hide those pages entirely.

## 2. Filter by Word Count (-fw) or Line Count (-fl)
If the file size fluctuates due to dynamic timestamps, filter by the number of words or lines instead:

# Ignore any response that has exactly 120 words or 45 lines
ffuf -u "URLPAYLOAD" -w wordlist.txt:PAYLOAD -w targets.txt:URL -fw 120 -fl 45

## 3. Autocalibration (-ac) — The Ultimate Weapon
Instead of calculating sizes manually, let ffuf do it. The -ac flag forces ffuf to send a few random, garbage requests at the start to map out the target's standard error pages, automatically filtering them out for the rest of the scan:

ffuf -w ~/bugbounty/SecLists/Fuzzing/XSS/XSS-Bypass-Strings.txt:PAYLOAD -w xss_targets.txt:URL -u "URLPAYLOAD" -ac

