## Part 2: Real-Time Bug Notifications
You do not want to stare at a terminal window for hours. You can connect ffuf directly to your Discord or Slack workspace using Notify (a tool by ProjectDiscovery).
## 1. Quick Notify Installation
Download the notify binary directly via GitHub release since we are on Linux:

curl -sOL https://github.com
tar -xvf notify_1.0.6_linux_amd64.tgz
sudo mv notify /usr/local/bin/
rm notify_1.0.6_linux_amd64.tgz provider-config.yaml

## 2. Configure Your Webhook
Generate a webhook URL inside your private Discord/Slack channel settings, then run this command to initialize your configuration file:

mkdir -p ~/.config/notify
nano ~/.config/notify/provider-config.yaml

Paste the following block into the file (replace with your actual webhook URL), then save and exit (Ctrl+O, Enter, Ctrl+X):

discord:
  - id: "bugbounty"
    discord_webhook_url: "https://discord.com"

## 3. Test the Notification Pipeline
Verify it works by sending a test message directly from your terminal to your chat app:

echo "Testing connection: Notify is operational!" | notify -id bugbounty

------------------------------
## Combining Both into an Unattended Fuzzing Pipeline
Now you can combine autocalibration, false-positive filtering, and live alerts into a single production pipeline. This script runs silently and pings your phone the exact second an anomaly or error reflection is found:

ffuf -w ~/bugbounty/SecLists/Fuzzing/Databases/SQLI.txt:PAYLOAD \
     -w juicy_parameters.txt:URL \
     -u "URLPAYLOAD" \
     -mr "SQL syntax|mysql|Error" \
     -ac -s | notify -id bugbounty


* -s: Silent mode (stops ffuf from printing progress bars, ensuring only the valid bugs get sent down the pipe to notify).

